1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,433
Welcome to School Computer Science,
Explained. This is AP Computer Science

2
00:00:05,433 --> 00:00:11,306
Principles, Big Idea 2: Data. Let's see
how computers store data, shrink it, and

3
00:00:11,306 --> 00:00:13,141
turn it into information.

4
00:00:14,315 --> 00:00:18,710
Everything a computer stores, numbers,
text, pictures, is made of bits.

5
00:00:19,160 --> 00:00:24,466
Big Idea 2 starts with how bits represent
numbers, and what happens when they run

6
00:00:24,466 --> 00:00:24,728
out.

7
00:00:25,878 --> 00:00:28,950
A bit is a zero or a one. A byte is eight
bits.

8
00:00:29,400 --> 00:00:35,073
In binary, each place is worth twice the
place to its right: one, two, four, eight,

9
00:00:35,073 --> 00:00:35,757
and so on.

10
00:00:36,907 --> 00:00:41,323
To convert binary to decimal, add the
place values where there's a one.

11
00:00:41,773 --> 00:00:45,997
One zero one one is eight plus two plus
one, which is eleven.

12
00:00:47,147 --> 00:00:50,368
With n bits, you can make two to the n
different patterns.

13
00:00:50,818 --> 00:00:56,397
Three bits give eight patterns, zero to
seven. Eight bits give two hundred fifty

14
00:00:56,397 --> 00:00:58,349
six, zero to two fifty five.

15
00:00:59,499 --> 00:01:02,870
Many languages store numbers in a fixed
number of bits.

16
00:01:03,320 --> 00:01:08,723
If a result is too big to fit, you get an
overflow error. With four bits, fifteen

17
00:01:08,723 --> 00:01:12,258
plus one doesn't fit, because the largest
is fifteen.

18
00:01:13,408 --> 00:01:18,712
Bits are also used for rounding. Some
numbers, like one third, can't be stored

19
00:01:18,712 --> 00:01:22,112
exactly, so computers store a close
approximation.

20
00:01:22,562 --> 00:01:28,539
That's why point one plus point two can
come out as point three zero zero zero

21
00:01:28,539 --> 00:01:30,072
zero zero zero four.

22
00:01:31,222 --> 00:01:34,976
Your turn. How many different values can
be stored with six bits?

23
00:01:35,426 --> 00:01:36,706
Pause the video now.

24
00:01:37,156 --> 00:01:41,722
Two to the sixth, which is sixty four
values, from zero to sixty three.

25
00:01:42,172 --> 00:01:45,116
Next, we'll compare lossless and lossy
compression.

26
00:01:46,265 --> 00:01:51,608
A raw photo from your phone can be dozens
of megabytes. Compression makes files

27
00:01:51,608 --> 00:01:55,396
smaller, so they're faster to send and
cheaper to store.

28
00:01:55,846 --> 00:01:57,019
There are two kinds.

29
00:01:58,169 --> 00:02:03,161
Lossless compression shrinks a file so it
can be restored exactly, bit for bit.

30
00:02:03,611 --> 00:02:09,926
One simple method is run length encoding.
A A A A B B B becomes four A, three B.

31
00:02:11,076 --> 00:02:16,398
Lossless compression is needed when every
bit matters: text documents, code, and

32
00:02:16,398 --> 00:02:17,262
spreadsheets.

33
00:02:17,712 --> 00:02:19,931
Zip files use lossless compression.

34
00:02:21,081 --> 00:02:26,137
Lossy compression throws away some data
that people are unlikely to notice.

35
00:02:26,587 --> 00:02:31,430
It makes files much smaller, but the
original can never be fully restored.

36
00:02:32,580 --> 00:02:38,468
Photos, music and video often use lossy
compression, like JPEG and MP3.

37
00:02:38,918 --> 00:02:43,568
A slightly blurrier photo is usually fine,
if it's ten times smaller.

38
00:02:44,718 --> 00:02:50,286
On the AP exam, choose lossless when the
original must be reconstructed exactly.

39
00:02:50,736 --> 00:02:55,728
Choose lossy when smaller size matters
most, and some quality loss is OK.

40
00:02:56,878 --> 00:03:02,788
Your turn. Use run length encoding to
compress W W W W W B B W.

41
00:03:03,238 --> 00:03:04,518
Pause the video now.

42
00:03:04,968 --> 00:03:07,314
Five W, two B, one W.

43
00:03:07,764 --> 00:03:10,346
Next, we'll extract information from data.

44
00:03:11,496 --> 00:03:15,720
Data is just raw values. Information is
what we learn from it.

45
00:03:16,170 --> 00:03:20,885
Computers help us turn huge amounts of
data into useful information.

46
00:03:22,035 --> 00:03:25,768
A store records every sale: the item, the
price, the time.

47
00:03:26,218 --> 00:03:31,920
One sale tells you little. A million sales
can show which items are popular, and when

48
00:03:31,920 --> 00:03:32,725
people shop.

49
00:03:33,875 --> 00:03:36,392
Patterns in data can lead to new
knowledge.

50
00:03:36,842 --> 00:03:42,481
But a pattern, or correlation, does not
prove that one thing causes another. Ice

51
00:03:42,481 --> 00:03:48,190
cream sales and sunburns rise together,
but ice cream doesn't cause sunburns. Hot

52
00:03:48,190 --> 00:03:49,599
weather causes both.

53
00:03:50,749 --> 00:03:54,440
Big data sets are often too large to
explore by hand.

54
00:03:54,890 --> 00:04:00,333
Programs can sort, filter and summarize
them, and scale to handle more data with

55
00:04:00,333 --> 00:04:01,354
more computers.

56
00:04:02,504 --> 00:04:07,690
Data can also be misleading. If it's
incomplete, or collected from only one

57
00:04:07,690 --> 00:04:10,248
group, the conclusions can be biased.

58
00:04:10,698 --> 00:04:14,431
More data doesn't fix bias if it's the
same kind of data.

59
00:04:15,581 --> 00:04:20,853
So when you see a claim based on data,
ask: where did the data come from, who is

60
00:04:20,853 --> 00:04:23,752
missing, and is it a correlation or a
cause?

61
00:04:24,902 --> 00:04:29,978
Your turn. A study finds students who eat
breakfast get higher grades. Does that

62
00:04:29,978 --> 00:04:32,326
prove breakfast causes higher grades?

63
00:04:32,776 --> 00:04:34,056
Pause the video now.

64
00:04:34,506 --> 00:04:39,690
No. It's a correlation. Something else,
like sleep or routine, could explain both.

65
00:04:40,140 --> 00:04:44,279
Next, we'll use programs to clean, filter
and visualize data.

66
00:04:45,429 --> 00:04:49,120
Before data can answer questions, it
usually needs work.

67
00:04:49,570 --> 00:04:53,026
Programs help us clean it, filter it, and
visualize it.

68
00:04:54,176 --> 00:04:56,842
Cleaning data makes it uniform and
correct.

69
00:04:57,292 --> 00:05:03,287
For example, a survey has the answers N Y,
New York, and new york. Cleaning turns

70
00:05:03,287 --> 00:05:08,764
them all into New York. Cleaning also
removes duplicates and fixes obvious

71
00:05:08,764 --> 00:05:09,282
errors.

72
00:05:10,432 --> 00:05:13,077
Filtering keeps only the rows you care
about.

73
00:05:13,527 --> 00:05:17,538
From a table of students, filter to only
the ones in grade ten.

74
00:05:18,688 --> 00:05:23,584
Then you can transform and summarize:
count, total, average, or find the

75
00:05:23,584 --> 00:05:24,128
maximum.

76
00:05:24,578 --> 00:05:29,684
What's the average score of grade ten
students? A program can answer that in a

77
00:05:29,684 --> 00:05:32,172
split second, even for a million rows.

78
00:05:33,322 --> 00:05:35,946
Visualizing turns numbers into charts.

79
00:05:36,396 --> 00:05:41,853
A bar chart compares categories. A line
chart shows change over time. Patterns

80
00:05:41,853 --> 00:05:45,420
that are hidden in a table can jump out in
a chart.

81
00:05:46,570 --> 00:05:51,476
Tools like spreadsheets, and programs you
write yourself, let you repeat these

82
00:05:51,476 --> 00:05:53,802
steps, and ask new questions quickly.

83
00:05:54,952 --> 00:06:00,601
Your turn. A data set has the colors red,
Red, and RED. Which step fixes this:

84
00:06:00,601 --> 00:06:03,208
cleaning, filtering, or visualizing?

85
00:06:03,658 --> 00:06:04,938
Pause the video now.

86
00:06:05,388 --> 00:06:10,402
Cleaning. It makes the values uniform, so
they're all counted as the same color.

87
00:06:10,852 --> 00:06:12,708
Next, we'll learn about metadata.

88
00:06:13,857 --> 00:06:16,737
Every photo you take stores more than the
picture.

89
00:06:17,187 --> 00:06:20,942
It also stores data about the picture.
That's metadata.

90
00:06:22,092 --> 00:06:24,182
Metadata is data about data.

91
00:06:24,632 --> 00:06:30,098
For a photo, metadata can include the
date, the time, the camera model, the

92
00:06:30,098 --> 00:06:32,867
image size, and even the GPS location.

93
00:06:34,017 --> 00:06:36,961
Metadata helps us organize and find data.

94
00:06:37,411 --> 00:06:43,503
Your phone uses it to group photos by date
and place. Search engines use it to find

95
00:06:43,503 --> 00:06:45,411
songs by artist and album.

96
00:06:46,561 --> 00:06:50,764
Changing or deleting metadata doesn't
change the main data.

97
00:06:51,214 --> 00:06:56,504
If you remove the location from a photo,
the picture itself looks exactly the same.

98
00:06:57,654 --> 00:07:03,286
But metadata can reveal a lot. A photo's
location can show where someone lives.

99
00:07:03,736 --> 00:07:08,280
Before you post photos publicly, consider
turning off location data.

100
00:07:09,430 --> 00:07:14,767
On the AP exam, look for questions that
ask which question can be answered using

101
00:07:14,767 --> 00:07:18,369
only the metadata, without looking at the
data itself.

102
00:07:19,519 --> 00:07:24,955
Your turn. A music file's metadata has the
title, artist, and length. Can you use

103
00:07:24,955 --> 00:07:30,324
only the metadata to find which songs are
longer than four minutes? Can you find

104
00:07:30,324 --> 00:07:32,404
which songs have a guitar solo?

105
00:07:32,854 --> 00:07:34,134
Pause the video now.

106
00:07:34,584 --> 00:07:39,411
Yes for the length. No for the guitar
solo: that's in the sound itself, not the

107
00:07:39,411 --> 00:07:39,960
metadata.

108
00:07:40,410 --> 00:07:46,213
That's the end of Big Idea two. Next, Big
Idea three: algorithms and programming.

109
00:07:47,363 --> 00:07:52,040
If this helped, subscribe to School
Computer Science, Explained, so you don't

110
00:07:52,040 --> 00:07:53,315
miss the next lesson.

111
00:07:53,765 --> 00:07:54,661
See you there.

